<!-- <p> <img src="../images/kMoria/pl6.gif" alt="preferences"> -->
<p> <img src="../images/kMoria/prefs.gif" alt="preferences"> </p>

<p> The preferences screen also allows you to: </p>
<ul>
<li>
Set the default font size ("big" normal font, or teensy flyspeck font.)
You can still change it at whim with '/F', but it will tend to start out
using the font size you select here.
<li>
Turn on/off "relative movement".  If this is on, the pie/donut is
centered around the @@; if this is off, the pie/donut is fixed in place,
centered on the screen.
<li>
Turn on/off "circles".  This is experimental.  It will draw the
borders of the movement donut, overlaid on the dungeon view.  This
might be helpful or a distraction.
    <br> <img src="../images/kMoria/circles.gif" alt="[concentric-circles screen]">
<li>
Turn on/off sound.  There are two places to turn it off.  One is "Game
Sound" under "General" in the PalmOS "Prefs" application.  (That
will turn off sound for all games that bother to pay attention.)  The
other is the "Sound" checkbox you see here.  If either one of these is
'off', kMoria will be silent; if you want sound, therefore, they must
both be 'on'.  Not that I have actually put any sound into the game yet.
<li>
    Turn on "invert" - if this is on, the background will be black,
    and everything in the dungeon will be drawn in white.
<li>
    Turn on "color" - this is only possible (visible) if you've chosen to
    <a href="color.php">define a set of colors</a>.
    If it is visible, it will appear below "invert".
    
    <li>
    Turn off "running" altogether, so that you move only one square at
a time no matter how far from the center you tap.  Then the following
options will not be of interest.
    <ul>
<li>
Turn off "cut known corners".  When you are running, kMoria is smart
enough to cut corners in twisty passages that you have previously
explored.  But maybe you don't want it to.
<li>
Turn off "examine potential corners".  When you are running,
kMoria assumes you do not want to cut a suspected corner in a twisty
passage that you have not previously explored.  But maybe you know better.
<li>
Turn on "stop when map sector changes".  When running, this will cause
you to stop running as soon you have run onto the next screen: a cautious
player will turn this option on in case there were monsters just out of sight.
<li>
Turn on "run through open doors".  You may want to stop to close them.
Or, you may want to stop to search (where there is one door, often there
are more doors that are concealed).  If you don't want to do these things,
you can turn this option off.
        </ul>
<li>
Turn on "prompt to pick up objects".  If you don't want to pick up
    absolutely every object you encounter, you would turn this on.
<li>
Turn on "highlight mineral seams".  This is an option for those who like
digging.  It will display the weaker types of mineral as <b>%</b>;
the ones you probably don't want to bother tunneling through are
still <b>#</b>.  Watch out for cave-ins.
    <li> You can also move the "stats" bar to the top of the screen.
</ul>
<p>
Not offered: "centered" scrolling to keep the rogue in the center of
the screen.  If you have played Unix Moria, you know that
even there the dungeon levels are divided into panels (to fit in your
xterm) and you move about within the panels.  Playing on your handheld
is just the same (except that the panels are smaller) so call this
"an authentic experience" and call me "lazy" </p>


